      <outline description="" text="Podcasts - History, Philosophy, etc.">
        <outline description="Scott and William put aside their continental differences to teach a history lesson to you (and hopefully a guest) each week in their attempt to come to a greater understanding about history, ideology and current affairs while having lots of laughs along the way." encoding="UTF-8" text="History Homos" title="History Homos" type="rss" version="RSS" xmlUrl="https://anchor.fm/s/2431f8ac/podcast/rss" rssguard:postProcess="" rssguard:xmlUrlType="0" xmlns:rssguard="https://github.com/martinrotter/rssguard"/>
        <outline description="The Martyr Made Podcast" encoding="UTF-8" text="The Martyr Made Podcast" title="The Martyr Made Podcast" type="rss" version="RSS" xmlUrl="https://feeds.blubrry.com/feeds/martyrmade_podcast.xml" rssguard:postProcess="" rssguard:xmlUrlType="0" xmlns:rssguard="https://github.com/martinrotter/rssguard"/>
        <outline description="Where history and epic collide--&quot;History on Fire&quot; is a podcast by author and university professor Daniele Bolelli." encoding="UTF-8" text="History on Fire" title="History on Fire" type="rss" version="RSS" xmlUrl="https://rss.introcast.io/1038514498/feeds.megaphone.fm/TPG8904827717" rssguard:postProcess="" rssguard:xmlUrlType="0" xmlns:rssguard="https://github.com/martinrotter/rssguard"/>
        <outline description="With millions of downloads, hundreds of hours of soundtracked content, and an overall emphasis on the cultural history behind famous works of literature, Literature and History is one of the most popular independent podcasts on its subject. Starting with Sumerian cuneiform in 3,100 BCE, Literature and History moves forward in chronological order through Assyriology, Egyptology, the Old Testament, Ancient Greece and Rome, the birth of Christianity, and the early Middle Ages. The show's current season is on early Islamic history.&#xa;&#xa;A typical episode (they average about two hours) features a general introduction to a work of literature, then a full summary of that work that expects no prior knowledge, and finally, an analysis of the cultural, biographical, and historical forces that gave rise to the work in question. Original symphonic and ambient background music is woven throughout each show, and all episodes offer free full, illustrated, footnoted transcriptions as well as quizzes for purposes of review. The show has no advertisements, and its host takes pride in a professional approach that avoids chitchat and ephemera and gets straight to the educational content. You can listen to the episodes in any order, although most listeners begin at the beginning and proceed from there, as the podcast itself is chronologically organized.&#xa;&#xa;Doug Metzger finished his Ph.D. in literature in 2011. His chief scholarly interest, following his dissertation work, continues to be 19th-century realism and postbellum American philosophy." encoding="UTF-8" text="Literature and History" title="Literature and History" type="rss" version="RSS" xmlUrl="https://rss.libsyn.com/shows/77861/destinations/351744.xml" rssguard:postProcess="" rssguard:xmlUrlType="0" xmlns:rssguard="https://github.com/martinrotter/rssguard"/>
        <outline description="Interviews, musings and extra material from the makers of Dan Carlin's Hardcore History.  If it did not fit in the HH feed it's probably here" encoding="UTF-8" text="Dan Carlin's Hardcore History: Addendum" title="Dan Carlin's Hardcore History: Addendum" type="rss" version="RSS" xmlUrl="https://rss.libsyn.com/shows/109255/destinations/596146.xml" rssguard:postProcess="" rssguard:xmlUrlType="0" xmlns:rssguard="https://github.com/martinrotter/rssguard"/>
        <outline description="In &quot;Hardcore History&quot; journalist and broadcaster Dan Carlin takes his &quot;Martian&quot;, unorthodox way of thinking and applies it to the past. Was Alexander the Great as bad a person as Adolf Hitler? What would Apaches with modern weapons be like? Will our modern civilization ever fall like civilizations from past eras? This isn't academic history (and Carlin isn't a historian) but the podcast's unique blend of high drama, masterful narration and Twilight Zone-style twists has entertained millions of listeners." encoding="UTF-8" text="Dan Carlin's Hardcore History" title="Dan Carlin's Hardcore History" type="rss" version="RSS" xmlUrl="http://feeds.feedburner.com/dancarlin/history?format=xml" rssguard:postProcess="" rssguard:xmlUrlType="0" xmlns:rssguard="https://github.com/martinrotter/rssguard"/>
        <outline description="Big braned content on all topics." encoding="UTF-8" text="Not Related! A Big-Braned Podcast" title="Not Related! A Big-Braned Podcast" type="rss" version="RSS" xmlUrl="https://notrelated.xyz/rss" rssguard:postProcess="" rssguard:xmlUrlType="0" xmlns:rssguard="https://github.com/martinrotter/rssguard"/>
      </outline>
